      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Low density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol is a causative factor in coronary artery disease, and LDL cholesterol lowering by statins is a cornerstone in cardiovascular risk management. Current guidelines recommend serum LDL cholesterol <70 mg/dl for patients at very high risk, in particular for those with coronary arterial disease (CAD) or type 2 diabetes (T2DM). We aimed to investigate the proportion of patients achieving current lipid goals in two high-risk cohorts of CAD patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "We obtained lipid profiles for two cohorts of patients who underwent coronary angiography for the evaluation of previously (>1 month) established CAD in 1999-2000 (n = 346) and in 2005-2007 (n = 850), at baseline and after 2 years of follow-up."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Overall, the proportion of patients with baseline LDL cholesterol <70 mg/dl was 6.1% in the first and 7.3% in the second cohort (p = 0.236). In logistic regression analysis, male gender (odds ratio [OR] 1.33, 95% confidence interval [CI] 1.11-2.96; p = 0.021) and presence of diabetes (OR 1.76, 95% CI 1.30-2.38; p <0.001) were independent predictors for reaching this LDL cholesterol treatment goal. After 2 years of follow-up, the proportion of patients with LDL cholesterol <70 mg/dl was 1.3% in the first and 9.5% in the second cohort (p <0.001 for the comparison between the two cohorts; p = 0.001 for the comparison of cohort I at baseline vs after 2 years and p = 0.001 for the comparison of cohort II at baseline vs after 2 years)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The proportion of CAD patients meeting current lipid treatment goals is low and has only slightly improved."
        }
      ]},
